      "title": "Moves 1-4 (positional_play)",
      "start_move": 1,
      "end_move": 2,
      "narrative": "The game begins like a classical Sicilian fight: White plays 1.e4 to claim central space and follow up with 3.Nf3, aiming to prepare d4 and quick piece development from g1 to f3. Black answers with 1...c5, immediately contesting the d4-square and turning the game toward asymmetrical play, and 3...d6 shores up the central e5-square while readying ...Nf6 and ...Nc6 or a kingside fianchetto. The critical idea was Black's early ...c5, which trimmed White's modest edge by challenging White's central ambitions, and 3...d6 further equalized the position by making Black's setup solid and coherent. From here both sides will pursue standard plans -- White seeks d4 and rapid development, Black seeks counterplay on the queenside and a resilient pawn structure.",
      "dominant_theme": "positional_play"
    }
  ],
  "game_narrative": "The game opened as a classical Sicilian: White grabbed central space with 1.e4 and rapid knight development while Black replied with 1...c5 and 3...d6 to immediately contest d4 and build a solid, asymmetrical structure. The critical phase revolved around whether White could achieve the d4 break and exploit faster development or whether Black's ...c5/...d6 setup would blunt White's ambitions and generate counterplay on the queenside. The ensuing middlegame featured typical Sicilian maneuvering  --  gambits for space and piece activity by White against patient pawn-structure-based counterplay by Black. The final result was decided when the central tension resolved: a successful d4 handed White long-term initiative, but if Black neutralized that break the game slipped into balanced, defensive play with chances for both sides.",
